I can't believe the amount of pointless bikeshedding that's already been do=
ne=0A=
over something that's going to be a rarely-if-ever used mechanism for one s=
et=0A=
of hardcore technical developers to communicate to another set of hardcore=
=0A=
technical developers.  This isn't a design for a multilingual IM system wit=
h=0A=
emojis and animated GIFs, it's a rarely-used debugging/diagnostic facility,=
=0A=
and yet we're arguing over whether a developer who can read a lengthy=0A=
technical document specified entirely in US-ASCII (TLS RFC) and implement i=
t=0A=
in C or Java (US-ASCII, English keywords) will be unable to communicate an=
=0A=
error message in anything but Cantonese (or Mandarin, or Qiang, or Kam=96Su=
i, or=0A=
Kipchak, or whatever was meant by "Chinese").=0A=
=0A=
Even for the few steps in the process where there's i18n available like the=
=0A=
gcc compile stage, the Chinese-speaking devs I know use the English version=
=0A=
because they don't want an attempted guess in another language at what the=
=0A=
error is, they want the actual error message from the compiler authors (man=
y=0A=
gcc error messages are barely comprehensible in English, let alone in an=0A=
Uighur translation.  Or maybe there are in Uighur, which is why I have trou=
ble=0A=
figuring out what they're saying).=0A=
=0A=
In any case it's a rarely-used, optional, by-special-request debugging=0A=
facility for technical developers, make it UTF-8 and the devs can decide wh=
at=0A=
they put in there.=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=0A=

